DTSTAMP:20260713T185438Z
DTSTART:20261207T163000Z
DTEND:20261208T013000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Global Health Research Methods Workshop
LOCATION:Stanford Research Park
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/global-health-research-methods-worksh
 op-2026
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53418302190186.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/glob
 al-health-research-methods-workshop-2026\n報名/票券: https://docs.goog
 le.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLSeq6JTQbPFLjaK4MlVNDR1emvOabtonfYKdn0-SFiAe7W8UsA/
 viewform\n\nThis two day all-day\, in-person workshop is for Stanford* Sch
 ool of Medicine fellows\, residents\, medical students\, and faculty inter
 ested in developing research skills applicable to global health. The works
 hop provides a basic overview and roadmap to help people interested in con
 ducting global health research develop their study question into a concept
  note that can form the basis for a study proposal.  The workshop focuses 
 on essential skills to develop a competitive proposal and budget\, includi
 ng how to approach global health research opportunities\, how to engage wi
 th collaborators\, consideration of sample size\, and analytical approach.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53418724495078@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261117T002716Z
DTSTART:20270605T220000Z
DTEND:20270606T000000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Workshop with Saif Azzuz and Elizabeth Azzuz
LOCATION:O'Donohue Educational Farm
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/workshop-with-saif-azzuz
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53418724495078.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/work
 shop-with-saif-azzuz\n報名/票券: https://thethirdplace.is/event/worksh
 op-with-saif-azzuz\n\nJoin us for an afternoon of artmaking with Saif Azzu
 z\, 2026-27 Doerr Visiting Artist\, and cultural fire practitioner Elizabe
 th Azzuz. In this all ages\, family friendly workshop\, the Azzuzs will sh
 are the significance of cultural fire\, or prescribed burns led by Indigen
 ous practitioners to steward healthy ecosystems. Participants will create 
 connections between native flora and fauna\, and make pigments and drawing
 s with charcoal from cultural fire burns.\n\nThis program takes place on t
 he O’Donohue Family Stanford Educational Farm at 555 Fremont Road\, Stan
 ford\, CA\, and is presented in conjunction with the Further Triennial exh
 ibition California Current\, on view at the Cantor from October 29\, 2026 
 through 2028\, which looks at the ecological\, infrastructural\, personal\
 , creative\, and activist networks that shape life in the region. Visit Ca
 lifornia Current to see works by Azzuz\, which include charcoal drawings m
 ade from similar processes.\n\nAll public programs at the Cantor Arts Cent
 er are always free! Space for this program is limited\; advance registrati
 on is recommended.\n\nRSVP here.\n\nAbout the Presenters\n\nSaif Azzuz (b.
  1987\, born in Eureka\, CA) is a Libyan-Yurok artist who resides in Pacif
 ica\, CA. He received a Bachelor’s Degree in Painting and Drawing from t
 he California College of the Arts in 2013. Working in painting\, sculpture
  and installation\, Azzuz’s work reimagines and examines our relationshi
 ps to nature\, privatization and commodification. The use of different med
 iums all
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53418849977084@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260713T202418Z
DTSTART:20270212T020000Z
DTEND:20270212T033000Z
SUMMARY:[現場]  Alexander Nemerov: The Work of Helen Frankenthaler
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center\, Cantor Eastman Auditorium
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/alexander-nemerov-the-work-of-helen-f
 rankenthaler
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53418849977084.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/alex
 ander-nemerov-the-work-of-helen-frankenthaler\n報名/票券: https://thet
 hirdplace.is/event/alexander-nemerov-the-work-of-helen-frankenthaler\n\nWh
 at is the relationship between an artwork and its maker? Can we confidentl
 y link the two\, or do they diverge in meaning and intention? Join us for 
 a keynote lecture by Stanford Art History Professor Alexander Nemerov\, au
 thor of Fierce Poise: Helen Frankenthaler and 1950s New York\, as he refle
 cts on these enduring questions. Professor Nemerov will focus on the print
 s of Helen Frankenthaler\, which were recently gifted to the Cantor Arts C
 enter by the Helen Frankenthaler Foundation as part of a major exhibition 
 on the artist’s work\, to explore her groundbreaking legacy.\n\n​RSVP 
 here.\n\nAbout the Speaker:\n\nAlexander Nemerov\, the Carl and Marilynn T
 homa Professor in the Arts and Humanities at Stanford\, is the author of m
 any books\, including most recently Fierce Poise: Helen Frankenthaler and 
 1950s New York\, and another book called The Forest: A Fable of America in
  the 1830s\, which the novelist Annie Proulx called “one of the richest 
 books ever to come my way\, achingly painful and astonishingly tender.” 
 At Stanford\, Nemerov teaches a popular course called “How to Look at Ar
 t and Why."\n\nAll public programs at the Cantor Arts Center are always fr
 ee! Space for this program is limited\; advance registration is recommende
 d. Those who have registered will have priority for seating.\n\n________\n
 \nAccessibility Information or Requests\n\nCantor Arts Center at Stanford 
 University is committed to ensuring our programs are accessible to everyon
 e. To request access information and/or accommodations 
END:VEVENT
