SUMMARY:[現場] Lunchtime Curator Talk | California Current: Art and Histo
 ries from the Bay Area
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center\, Freidenrich Family Gallery (221)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-lunchtime-curator-talk-califo
 rnia-current-art-and-histories-from-the-bay-area
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53801276792667.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/copy
 -of-lunchtime-curator-talk-california-current-art-and-histories-from-the-b
 ay-area\n報名/票券: https://thethirdplace.is/event/rgPHwqPV\n\nJoin Ma
 ggie Dethloff\, Assistant Curator of Photography and New Media\, on this s
 pecial tour of California Current: Art and Histories from the Bay Area.\n\
 nRSVP here.\n\nHow does a community stay rooted in a region that’s const
 antly changing? California Current invites you to consider this question a
 nd others through its more than 60 works — many on view for the first ti
 me — that explore the interplay between Bay Area history\, art\, and art
 ists over the past century.\n\nTitled after the major cold-water current t
 hat runs the length of the West Coast of North America\, California Curren
 t brings together paintings\, sculptures\, works on paper\, photographs\, 
 and time-based media by Bay Area artists from the twentieth century to the
  present. The exhibition traces a series of interconnected currents — ec
 ological\, infrastructural\, technological\, personal\, creative\, and pol
 itical — that have shaped both artistic production and everyday life in 
 Northern California.\n\nArranged thematically across time and media\, Cali
 fornia Current places historical works in conversation with contemporary o
 nes\, emphasizing both continuity and change. Together\, they ask: What ha
 s drawn so many people to this region? What has allowed communities and cr
 eative practices to take root here? And what continues to keep so many of 
 us — despite the Bay Area’s contradictions\, pressures\, and constant 
 reinvention — deeply connected to this place? Presented as part of the F
 urther Triennial\, this exhibition features works by arti
END:VEVENT
