UID:stanford-53853306172764@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261120T171352Z
DTSTART:20261204T200000Z
DTEND:20261204T200000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Who’s Afraid of the East Romans?
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/whos-afraid-of-the-east-romans
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53853306172764.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/whos
 -afraid-of-the-east-romans\n\nRecent years have seen a push to recognize t
 he east Roman state and its people for what and who they said they were 
 — Romans — and not to hide that reality under the invented term “Byz
 antium\,” which has always encoded negative stereotypes. In this talk\, 
 professor Kaldellis will discuss the ideologies and structural interests t
 hat have resisted acknowledging a Roman people in the east\, ranging from 
 western Eurocentrism to modern Greek nationalism. “Rome" has long been a
  foundational matrix for thinking about western identities and states: wha
 t would it look like if it made room to accommodate its eastern branch on 
 an equal basis?\n\nRSVP here.\n\nAnthony Kaldellis is a professor of Class
 ics at the University of Chicago. He has published widely on the history\,
  culture\, and literature of Byzantium\, ranging from the fourth to the fi
 fteenth centuries. His works include a new\, comprehensive history of the 
 eastern Roman empire: The New Roman Empire: A History of Byzantium (Oxford
 \, 2023). Most recently he has published a new history of the fall of Cons
 tantinople in 1453 and a study of western perceptions of Byzantium from la
 te antiquity to World War II (Phantom Byzantium). He has translated mediev
 al Greek authors\, including historians such as Prokopios\, Attaleiates\, 
 and Laonikos Chalkokondyles\, and is the host of the academic podcast “B
 yzantium & Friends\,” going strong since 2019.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53853336941939@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261103T215504Z
DTSTART:20270211T170000Z
DTEND:20270211T230000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Stanford Global and Planetary Health Research Convening
LOCATION:Frances C. Arrillaga Alumni Center Stanford\, CA
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/stanford-global-and-planetary-health-
 research-convening
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53853336941939.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/stan
 ford-global-and-planetary-health-research-convening\n報名/票券: https:
 //www.eventbrite.com/e/2027-stanford-global-and-planetary-health-research-
 convening-tickets-1998392877893?aff=oddtdtcreator\n\nAbout the Stanford Gl
 obal and Planetary Health Research Convening\n\nThe 2027 Global and Planet
 ary Health Research Convening will take place Thursday\, Feb. 11 from 9am 
 – 3pm at Stanford.\n\nThe Annual Global and Planetary Health Research Co
 nvening fosters discussion among researchers across a variety of disciplin
 es. We welcome participants to share their ideas and experiences with glob
 al and planetary health research and to explore new collaborations and opp
 ortunities for engagement. Prior convenings have attracted broad participa
 tion from physician-scientists\, engineers\, economists\, earth scientists
 \, ecologists\, and mathematical modelers.\n\nRegister here\n\nAbstract Su
 bmissions\n\nThe Stanford Center for Human and Planetary Health and the Ce
 nter for Innovation in Global Health invite everyone wishing to report on 
 original research or interventions to submit abstracts to be considered fo
 r presentation. This year’s theme is Global and Planetary Health. We wel
 come abstracts from a broad range of topics and disciplines related to the
 se topics. Apply below by Oct. 22 at 5pm PT. \n\nNote: Abstracts should be
  no longer than 1700 characters and must represent academic research.\n\nI
 f you have any questions\, please contact Yosefa Gilon at ygilon@stanford.
 edu.\n\nSubmit an abstract
END:VEVENT
