SUMMARY:[混合] HAI Seminar with Angèle Christin in Conversation with Han
 nah Zeavin
LOCATION:Gates Computer Science Building\, 119
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/hai-seminar-with-angele-christin-in-c
 onversation-with-hannah-zeavin
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53861598502050.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/hai-
 seminar-with-angele-christin-in-conversation-with-hannah-zeavin\n線上連
 結: https://stanford.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_oNm5zUqVS5WO0JywL4KyTQ#/
 registration\n報名/票券: https://hai.stanford.edu/events/angele-christ
 in-hannah-zeavin-gurus-hucksters-entertainers-how-influencers-reshaped-soc
 ial-media\n\nBook Launch: “Gurus\, Hucksters\, Entertainers: How Influen
 cers Reshaped Social Media”\n\nHAI Seminar with Angèle Christin in Conv
 ersation with Hannah Zeavin\n\nVisit our website to learn more about the e
 vent agenda\, speakers\, and other details\n\nPlease join us for an illumi
 nating investigation of the complex work of social media content creators
 —their lives\, conflicts\, and controversies. \n\nIn this eye-opening bo
 ok\, digital studies scholar Angèle Christin demystifies the content crea
 tor economy\, explaining its long history and the pitfalls of its professi
 onalization. Drawing on in-depth fieldwork with digital creators as varied
  as vegan YouTubers\, dads of Instagram\, and self-described drama comment
 ators\, Christin shows how individuals enter social media creation\, how t
 hey make money\, and how their online environments shape their content.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53862726309410@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260903T060649Z
DTSTART:20270115T010000Z
DTEND:20270115T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Eugene Wang: "Biocosmic Art\; or How to Capture a Painter
 ’s Brain?"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/eugene-wang-biocosmic-art-or-how-to-c
 apture-a-painters-brain
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53862726309410.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/euge
 ne-wang-biocosmic-art-or-how-to-capture-a-painters-brain\n\nAbstract:\n\nW
 hen medical imaging mapped the neural deterioration of the legendary Hong 
 Kong ink painter Irene Chou (1924-2011)\, she looked at her own brain X-ra
 ys and experienced a startling revelation: “I saw vital images of tree b
 ranches… that is life—my life.” In this lecture\, Eugene Wang (Harva
 rd University) reconsiders Chou’s art through the converging lenses of p
 lant sensience\, systems biology\, and Buddhist-cum-Daoist meditative prac
 tice\, revealing her paintings not as abstract landscapes but as experimen
 ts in biocosmic imagination. From the refuge of her father’s photographi
 c darkroom in Shanghai to her solitary\, obsessive sketches of “ten thou
 sand trees” in Hong Kong’s dense woods\, Chou turned ink into a means 
 of self-preservation: thousands of rhythmic parallel lines became protecti
 ve membranes\, holding an endangered inner world against chaos. Bringing C
 harles Darwin’s studies of plant movement\, Herbert Read’s modernist a
 esthetics\, medical imaging\, and the alchemical cosmology of breathing ex
 ercise into dialogue\, Wang shows how Chou transformed illness and vulnera
 bility into paintings of biocosmic imaginative reach—works that move fro
 m capillary and cell to vortex and galaxy\, forging a radiant visual lexic
 on of resilience\, renewal\, and cosmic liberation. \n\nBio:\n\nEugene Y. 
 Wang is the Abby Aldrich Rockefeller Professor of Asian Art at Harvard Uni
 versity. A Guggenheim Fellow and art-history editor of the Encyclopedia of
  Buddhism\, he is the author of Shaping the Lotus Su
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53862757496054@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260904T105640Z
DTSTART:20270129T010000Z
DTEND:20270129T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Anning Jing: "Borobudur and a Tantric Buddhist Maṇḍala
  Associated with Vajrabodhi (671–741)"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/anning-jing-borobudur-and-a-tantric-b
 uddhist-maala-associated-with-vajrabodhi-671-741
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53862757496054.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/anni
 ng-jing-borobudur-and-a-tantric-buddhist-maala-associated-with-vajrabodhi-
 671-741\n\nAbstract:\n\nThis talk reexamines Borobudur through the transmi
 ssion of Esoteric Buddhist teachings associated with Indian Tantric Buddhi
 st master Vajrabodhi (671–741). It argues that Vajrabodhi’s activities
  in Java and China\, his transmission of Tantric teachings to Amoghavajra\
 , and the subsequent initiation of the Javanese monk Bianhong into the Gar
 bhadhātu Maṇḍala by Amoghavajra’s disciple Huiguo created the histo
 rical and doctrinal framework for Borobudur’s construction. A three-dime
 nsional Buddhist maṇḍala known as the shi 式 or “secret disc”\, d
 escribed in a Tang Buddhist text attributed to Vajrabodhi\, closely parall
 els Borobudur’s architectural form\, cosmological model\, iconographic p
 rogram of the Five Jina Buddhas\, and solar symbolism. It proposes that Bo
 robudur was conceived as a monumental celestial maṇḍala of Mahāvairoc
 ana\, shaped by Vajrabodhi’s Esoteric Buddhist lineage and its cosmologi
 cal vision. Vajrabodhi’s shi\, however\, was rooted in the traditional C
 hinese shi\, whose origins can be traced to the Neolithic Liangzhu Culture
  of southeastern China.\n\nBio:\n\nAnning Jing received his MA in Art Hist
 ory from Ohio State University in 1989 and PhD in Art History from Princet
 on University in 1993. He has been teaching Asian art at Michigan State Un
 iversity since 1995. His main publications include The Water God's Temple 
 of the Guangsheng Monastery: Cosmic Function of Art\, Ritual\, and Theater
  (Leiden: Brill\, 2002)\, Yuandai biha “Shenxianfuhui tu” (The Yuan Mu
 rals “Assembly o
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54010921145793@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260918T222043Z
DTSTART:20270326T010000Z
DTEND:20270326T010000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Evening Curator Talk | California Current: Art and Histori
 es from the Bay Area
LOCATION:Cantor Arts Center\, Freidenrich Family Gallery (221)
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/copy-of-lunchtime-curator-talk-califo
 rnia-current-art-and-histories-from-the-bay-area-282
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54010921145793.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/copy
 -of-lunchtime-curator-talk-california-current-art-and-histories-from-the-b
 ay-area-282\n報名/票券: https://thethirdplace.is/event/gPgMztAP\n\nJoi
 n Maggie Dethloff\, Assistant Curator of Photography and New Media\, on th
 is special tour of California Current: Art and Histories from the Bay Area
 .\n\nRSVP here.\n\nHow does a community stay rooted in a region that’s c
 onstantly changing? California Current invites you to consider this questi
 on and others through its more than 60 works — many on view for the firs
 t time — that explore the interplay between Bay Area history\, art\, and
  artists over the past century.\n\nTitled after the major cold-water curre
 nt that runs the length of the West Coast of North America\, California Cu
 rrent brings together paintings\, sculptures\, works on paper\, photograph
 s\, and time-based media by Bay Area artists from the twentieth century to
  the present. The exhibition traces a series of interconnected currents 
 — ecological\, infrastructural\, technological\, personal\, creative\, a
 nd political — that have shaped both artistic production and everyday li
 fe in Northern California.\n\nArranged thematically across time and media\
 , California Current places historical works in conversation with contempo
 rary ones\, emphasizing both continuity and change. Together\, they ask: W
 hat has drawn so many people to this region? What has allowed communities 
 and creative practices to take root here? And what continues to keep so ma
 ny of us — despite the Bay Area’s contradictions\, pressures\, and con
 stant reinvention — deeply connected to this place? Presented as part of
  the Further Triennial\, this exhibition features works by arti
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-53870838804293@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261108T005423Z
DTSTART:20261205T170000Z
DTEND:20261205T230000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] "Tracing Mindfulness Back to Its Sources": T. T. and W. F.
  Chao Workshop with Bhikkhunī Dhammadinnā and Paul Harrison
LOCATION:Alumni Center\, McCaw Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/tracing-mindfulness-back-to-its-sourc
 es-t-t-and-w-f-chao-workshop-with-bhikkhuni-dhammadinna-and-paul-harrison
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/53870838804293.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/trac
 ing-mindfulness-back-to-its-sources-t-t-and-w-f-chao-workshop-with-bhikkhu
 ni-dhammadinna-and-paul-harrison\n\nThis workshop is a part of the T. T. &
  W. F. Chao Buddhism and Contemporary Society Series and will explore the 
 early Buddhist sources of mindfulness meditation in theory and in practice
 . Participants will be guided through an examination of the teachings that
  form the shared inheritance of all later Buddhist traditions and that als
 o inform many contemporary applications. Throughout the day\, we will be l
 ooking into multiple constructs and perspectives on mindfulness and their 
 practical implications\, discovering how theory\, practice\, and their int
 erweaving have continually evolved—shaped by diverse maps and goals of t
 he Buddhist path across time\, through different cultures\, and into prese
 nt-day recontextualizations.\n\n​The workshop will offer participants th
 e opportunity for experiential exploration of the relevant practices toget
 her with an accessible scholarly grounding in the primary textual sources 
 in translation.\n\n​This event is free and open to the public\, and ever
 yone is welcome. However\, because of space limitations\, registration is 
 essential. Registration will close on November 10. Some prior exposure to 
 basic Buddhist or mindfulness teachings and practices is helpful but not a
  prerequisite for participation.\n\nPlease register here. \n\n​Schedule:
 \n\n​Morning Session\n\n​09:00–09:10 Introduction\n\n​09:10–09:2
 0 Guided Meditation\n\n​09:20–10:00 The Four Foundations of Mindfulnes
 s (satipaṭṭhāna) in Theory & Practice: An Overview\n\n​10:00–10:3
 0 Discussion\n\n​10:30–10:45 Short Break\n\n
END:VEVENT
