DTEND:20261203T211500Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Ivetta Sergeeva — The Authoritarian Passport: How States
  Discipline Emigrants
LOCATION:https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWT
 pXWrc2R.1
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/ivetta-sergeeva-the-authoritarian-pas
 sport-how-states-discipline-emigrants-without-repressing-them
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54004106866444.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/ivet
 ta-sergeeva-the-authoritarian-passport-how-states-discipline-emigrants-wit
 hout-repressing-them\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/j/97375161734
 ?pwd=x4DaCfRGRry3yOGjLfhQbWTpXWrc2R.1\n\nCitizenship is conventionally und
 erstood as a source of rights and protection. It is also a source of depen
 dency: states retain distinctive authority over passports\, legal identiti
 es\, civil status\, and mobility that citizens need even when they live ab
 road. Under authoritarianism\, these dependencies become instruments of po
 litical control. The study introduces extraterritorial authoritarian citiz
 enship\, a continuing relationship in which emigrants depend on origin-sta
 te institutions whose provision of nominally universal rights is political
 ly conditional. It generates latent leverage: the capacity to impose costs
  without exercising it against anyone in particular. Emigrants may curb di
 ssent even when never targeted\, and transnational repression activates le
 verage already at work. Using a four-wave panel survey of 4\,000+ Russian 
 emigrants in over 100 countries and 500+ interviews\, the authors show tha
 t what emigrants fear is the open-ended legal and administrative reach of 
 the origin state rather than targeted violence\, that this fear suppresses
  contentious activism specifically\, and that only the protection of anoth
 er state reduces it. (In collaboration with Emil Kamalov and Karolina Nugu
 manova).\n\nABOUT THE SPEAKER\n\nIvetta Sergeeva specializes in comparativ
 e social science\, focusing on political behavior\, civil society\, citize
 nship and migration. In her research\, she employs a mixed-methods approac
 h\, emphasizing surveys\, statistical modeling\, experiments\, and intervi
 ews. Apar
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54036622167796@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20260923T003808Z
DTSTART:20270521T000000Z
DTEND:20270521T013000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Kurtis Schaeffer: "Poetry in Tibet: Pushing Boundaries of 
 Buddhist Language and Thought"
LOCATION:Lathrop Library\, 224
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/kurtis-schaeffer-poetry-in-tibet-push
 ing-boundaries-of-buddhist-language-and-thought
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54036622167796.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/kurt
 is-schaeffer-poetry-in-tibet-pushing-boundaries-of-buddhist-language-and-t
 hought\n\nAbstract:\n\nTibetan writers of kāvya-style poetry exploited th
 e structured flexibility of the medium to praise the heroes and ideals of 
 Buddhist tradition\, to innovate in both literary form and practices of th
 ought\, and at times even to transgress the strictures implicit in other g
 enres of Buddhist literature. This talk explores the popular genre of sing
 le verses intended to illustrate the poetic features described in Daṇḍ
 in's Mirror of Poetry\, and suggests that kāvya poetry was an important s
 pace in elite literature where pre-modern Tibetan writers could openly pus
 h the boundaries of Buddhist language\, thought\, and feeling.\n\n \n\nBio
 :\n\nKurtis Schaeffer is a student of Buddhist literature in Tibet and the
  Himalayas. He is the translator of The Life of the Buddha (2014) and Budd
 hist Meditation: Classic Teachings from Tibet (2024)\, both from Penguin C
 lassics\, and the author of Himalayan Hermitess (2004)\, The Culture of th
 e Book in Tibet (2009)\, and other books. He is the Frances Myers Ball Pro
 fessor in the Department of Religious Studies at the University of Virgini
 a.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54037437866563@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261108T005425Z
DTSTART:20261203T200000Z
DTEND:20261203T210000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Jana Hunter- The Child of Freedom: Temporal Sovereignty in
  Habsburg Bohemia
LOCATION:Encina Hall\, William J. Perry Conference Room\, Encina Hall Centr
 al\, 2nd Floor
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-child-of-freedom-temporal-soverei
 gnty-in-habsburg-bohemia
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54037437866563.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 child-of-freedom-temporal-sovereignty-in-habsburg-bohemia\n線上連結: h
 ttps://stanford.zoom.us/webinar/register/WN_mwn-XbggT_KUsVxkMnQ0xg\n報名
 /票券: https://luma.com/pd3ig2ef\n\nWho decides what time it is? In Habs
 burg Bohemia\, that question drew in nearly every other: nation and empire
 \, identity and sovereignty\, capital and a newly connected world. Railway
 s and telegraphs created a time shared across distance\, and with it a deb
 ate over who had the power to set it. Histories of time standardization ha
 ve told a story of unification\; histories of Habsburg sovereignty\, meanw
 hile\, have located its conflicts in diets and parliaments. This talk brin
 gs them together\, asking how an empire tried to synchronize every local c
 lock\, where authority over time was located\, and why time became a quest
 ion of sovereignty. After 1867\, Bohemia's unresolved constitutional statu
 s moved into the infrastructure of daily life: whose observatory produced 
 noon\, which meridian ran the trains\, whose clocks kept the time. At stak
 e was not only who ruled Bohemia\, but how time could be controlled at all
 . \n\nBy the turn of the century\, Bohemians called their divided time dua
 lismus\, the word for the constitutional settlement itself. This talk trac
 es these jurisdictional disputes over four decades from railway decrees to
  universal time. The lines of conflict did not run simply between empire a
 nd nation: German liberals\, Czech nationalists\, intellectuals\, and busi
 nessmen appealed to historic rights\, national identity\, commercial inter
 est\, and civic embarrassment. Jurisdiction over time was scalar\, dispers
 ed across ministries\, railway companies\, international conferences\, and
  municipal c
END:VEVENT
