UID:stanford-54047926358295@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261001T203932Z
DTSTART:20261215T000000Z
DTEND:20261215T013000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] YoungJu Jo | Data-driven discovery and control of neural c
 omputations
LOCATION:Stanford Neurosciences Building\, James Lin and Nisa Leung Seminar
  Room\, E153
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/cnds-seminar-youngju-jo
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54047926358295.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/cnds
 -seminar-youngju-jo\n\nAbstract\n\nThe brain is a nonlinear dynamical syst
 em whose evolving population activity gives rise to computations that guid
 e behavior. We will present a data-driven systems neuroscience framework t
 hat integrates large-scale neurophysiology\, interpretable dynamical syste
 ms modeling\, and targeted perturbations in cognitive animal models. This 
 approach has enabled the discovery of cell-type-specific and brain-wide dy
 namical structures underlying reward and memory computations\, including a
 ttractor manifolds that integrate past rewards and steer future decision-m
 aking. To enable causal tests and model-guided control of these computatio
 ns\, we have developed optical and genetic technologies for cellular-resol
 ution\, bidirectional control of neural populations\, along with optimal c
 ontrol approaches for designing perturbations. Together\, these converging
  advances set the stage for a closed-loop strategy to measure\, model\, an
 d control neural dynamics and computations\, toward the long-term vision o
 f writing complex information into the brain.\n\nBio \n\nYoungJu Jo is an 
 Assistant Professor and the Brody Family Faculty Fellow in the Department 
 of Electrical Engineering and Computer Sciences and the Helen Wills Neuros
 cience Institute at the University of California\, Berkeley. His laborator
 y develops and applies next-generation neurotechnologies to measure\, mode
 l\, and control neural dynamics and computations in behaving animals. He c
 ompleted his postdoctoral training in Bioengineering in 2026\, 
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54072728872237@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261112T004703Z
DTSTART:20270203T010000Z
DTEND:20270203T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Artist Salon: North African Traditions\, New Music Creatin
 g a Concerto for Oud and Baroque Orchestra
LOCATION:Building 80\, 102
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/artist-salon-north-african-traditions
 -new-music-creating-a-concerto-for-oud-and-baroque-orchestra
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54072728872237.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/arti
 st-salon-north-african-traditions-new-music-creating-a-concerto-for-oud-an
 d-baroque-orchestra\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAI
 pQLSfgP3t8lQvY7_dQbsN-N-2J6dlH37dh9r5X0Ot0QxSxNHGhow/closedform\n\nJoin us
  for an Artist Salon: North African Traditions\, New Music Creating a Conc
 erto for Oud and Baroque Orchestra\, a free public conversation presented 
 by Philharmonia Baroque Orchestra & Chorale\, Stanford Live\, and Stanford
 's Department of African and African American Studies.\n\nDiscover the sto
 ry of the oud—the fretless\, pear-shaped lute whose sound has shaped mus
 ical traditions across North Africa and the Middle East for centuries and 
 which is an ancestor of the modern guitar.\n\nComposer Tarik O'Regan and o
 ud virtuoso Joseph Tawadros will take audiences behind the scenes of a new
  piece O’Regan has composed for oud and Baroque orchestra\, scheduled to
  premiere at Bing Concert Hall on February 7\, 2027.\n\nO'Regan\, whose mu
 sic has been performed by leading ensembles around the world\, will discus
 s the inspiration behind the work\, while Tawadros—whose performances ha
 ve introduced international audiences to the expressive sound of the oud
 —will share the instrument's rich musical traditions\, demonstrate its r
 emarkable range\, and offer insights into bringing this new composition to
  life. The conversation will be moderated by Stanford professor and acclai
 med scholar Professor Matthew D. Morrison\, whose work explores music\, cu
 lture\, and identity.\n\nThe event also features live musical demonstratio
 n by Joseph Tawadros\, a performance by the Stanford Middle Eastern Ensemb
 le\, and an audience Q&A\, offering attendees the opportunity to engage di
 rectly with the artists.\n\nDate: Tuesday February 
END:VEVENT
