SUMMARY:[混合] Fireside Chat with Anne Wojcicki
LOCATION:Assembly Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/fireside-chat-with-anne-wojcicki-7984
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54178864866614.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/fire
 side-chat-with-anne-wojcicki-7984\n線上資訊:\nAccess dial in details a
 t https://medicine.stanford.edu/education/grand-rounds.html\n\nStanford D
 epartment of Medicine would like to invite all Stanford community members 
 to attend this instance of the Medicine Grand Rounds (MGR) series\, which 
 will feature Anne Wojcicki\, a pioneering force in consumer genomics and p
 ersonalized medicine who co-founded 23andMe to democratize access to genet
 ic information\, and who is now leading its next chapter through the not-f
 or-profit TTAM Research Institute.\n\nCE accreditation info here.\n\nCME A
 ctivity ID: 58654\n\nMOC Credit (1): Complete this form after each session
 \; reflective statement (1-2 sentences) required.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54178976599441@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261007T180235Z
DTSTART:20261201T233000Z
DTEND:20261202T003000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Applied Physics/Physics Colloquium: Jo Dunkley - "The Simo
 ns Observatory"
LOCATION:Hewlett Teaching Center\, 201
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/applied-physicsphysics-colloquium-jo-
 dunkley-the-simons-observatory
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54178976599441.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/appl
 ied-physicsphysics-colloquium-jo-dunkley-the-simons-observatory\n\nAbstrac
 t: The Simons Observatory\, a new millimeter-wave observatory in the Ataca
 ma\, is now gathering data. With its smaller telescopes we are seeking a p
 rimordial signal imprinted by gravitational waves generated in the early u
 niverse. Its largest telescope is measuring the finer scales of the CMB wi
 th increased sensitivity\, probing the initial conditions of the early uni
 verse as well as revealing the later-formed cosmic web of gas and dark mat
 ter through the gravitational lensing and Sunyaev Zel’dovich effects. It
  will map half the sky at arcminute resolution\, overlapping with a number
  of large-scale structure surveys including Rubin. By surveying half the s
 ky every couple of days\, we also hope to see new types of transient astro
 nomical events in millimeter-wavelengths.  I will talk about the current s
 tatus of SO\, and its science plans.\n\nJo Dunkley is the Joseph Henry Pro
 fessor of Physics and Astrophysical Sciences at Princeton University. She 
 works on the analysis and interpretation of cosmological observations. She
  is currently the Spokesperson for the Simons Observatory collaboration. P
 reviously she was a member of NASA's WMAP science team and a scientist on 
 the European Space Agency’s Planck satellite project\, and was the analy
 sis leader for the Atacama Cosmology Telescope. She is a Fellow of the Roy
 al Society.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54179718433669@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261108T005425Z
DTSTART:20261204T023000Z
DTEND:20261204T050000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Film Screening of the Latvian submission for the Academy A
 wards – Ulya
LOCATION:Bishop Auditorium
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/film-screening-of-ulya
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54179718433669.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/film
 -screening-of-ulya\n報名/票券: https://docs.google.com/forms/d/e/1FAIp
 QLScpwdybItslBRJvAh6uE31N4z7nWhDvy18n7TLD4zTGPMCoJw/viewform\n\nJoin us fo
 r a film screening of Latvia's submission for the Academy Awards and comme
 ntary from Dr. Karla Oeler\, Director of the Center for Russian\, Eastern 
 European\, and Eurasian Studies and Associate Professor of Film and Media 
 Studies in the Department of Art & Art History.\n\nUlya (2026) is a true s
 tory about Uljana Semjonova\, who is a very tall girl living on a remote f
 arm in Soviet Latvia in 1964. Her height\, almost two meters\, worries her
  family\, who aren't sure how she will fit into the world. When her class 
 photo reaches basketball coaches\, she is taken to Riga to train with TTT 
 Riga. She works hard but feels torn\, because basketball doesn't give her 
 the ordinary life she dreams of. After running away back home\, she realiz
 es that both the village and the sport see her only as "the tall girl." Wh
 en she finally accepts herself\, she must decide whether to stay hidden or
  try to become the best basketball player in the world.\n\nDr. Karla Oeler
  is the Director of the Center for Russian\, Eastern European\, and Eurasi
 an Studies and an Associate Professor of Film and Media Studies in the Dep
 artment of Art & Art History. Her research and teaching interests are film
  history\, theory and criticism\, with emphases including the theory and p
 ractice of Sergei Eisenstein\, cinematic representations of violence and t
 hinking\, and the relations between film aesthetics\, literature\, theory 
 and philosophy. She is the author of A Grammar of Murder:  Violent Scenes 
 and Film Form (University of Chic
END:VEVENT
