SUMMARY:[現場] Slavic Colloquium: Julia Verkholantsev: Etymology as Histo
 rical Knowledge in Medieval and Early Modern Historiography: Why Libuše M
 arried a Ploughman and Wanda Refused a Foreign King
LOCATION:Building 260\, Pigott Hall\, Rm. 216
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/slavic-colloquium-julia-verkholantsev
 -etymology-as-historical-knowledge-in-medieval-and-early-modern-historiogr
 aphy-why-libuse-married-a-ploughman-and-wanda-refused-a-foreign-king
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54294878918166.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/slav
 ic-colloquium-julia-verkholantsev-etymology-as-historical-knowledge-in-med
 ieval-and-early-modern-historiography-why-libuse-married-a-ploughman-and-w
 anda-refused-a-foreign-king\n\nPlease join the upcoming Slavic Colloquium 
 event. Julia Verkholantsev (University of Pennsylvania) will present a tal
 k titled "Etymology as Historical Knowledge in Medieval and Early Modern H
 istoriography: Why Libuše Married a Ploughman and Wanda Refused a Foreign
  King."\n\nFor premodern historians\, names could contain knowledge about 
 the past. The art of etymological interpretation\, inherited from classica
 l antiquity\, offered a means of uncovering that knowledge\, especially wh
 en it came to explaining predestination and corroborating orally transmitt
 ed stories about origins\, genealogies\, and political order. Focusing on 
 the legendary female rulers Libuše in the Czech and Wanda in the Polish c
 hronicle traditions\, this talk examines how interpretations of their name
 s helped shape narratives about gender\, rulership\, marriage\, and legal 
 and political authority. By tracing how meanings uncovered through etymolo
 gical analysis generated narrative possibilities\, the talk presents histo
 rians’ recourse to etymology not simply as linguistic wordplay\, but as 
 a means of establishing historical truth and making sense of the past.
END:VEVENT
