SUMMARY:[線上] Lower-Carb Makeovers: Carb Counting and Smart Swaps
LOCATION:https://healthlibrary.stanford.edu/lectures-events.html#carb-count
 ing
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/lower-carb-makeovers-carb-counting-an
 d-smart-swaps
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54322589080002.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/lowe
 r-carb-makeovers-carb-counting-and-smart-swaps\n線上連結: https://heal
 thlibrary.stanford.edu/lectures-events.html#carb-counting\n報名/票券: 
 https://healthlibrary.stanford.edu/lectures-events.html#carb-counting\n\nJ
 essica Shipley\, MS\, RD\, CDCES\, presents a practical session on reducin
 g dietary carbohydrates to support glycemic control. Learn carb counting\,
  portion control\, label reading\, lower-carb substitutions for common sta
 ples\, sample meal ideas\, and strategies to tailor choices to individual 
 needs and preferences.\n\nJessica Shipley\, MS\, RD\, CDCES\, is a registe
 red dietitian and certified diabetes care and education specialist at Stan
 ford Health Care.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54323002574194@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261024T005438Z
DTSTART:20270127T200000Z
DTEND:20270127T210000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Privileged Minorities: A History of Wealth Concentration i
 n South Africa - Mesrob Vartavarian
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/privileged-minorities-a-history-of-we
 alth-concentration-in-south-africa-mesrob-vartavarian
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54323002574194.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/priv
 ileged-minorities-a-history-of-wealth-concentration-in-south-africa-mesrob
 -vartavarian\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/dDxgxoZ7Bfqzvzy89\n\nSessio
 n Description:\n\nThis book examines a central paradox in South African hi
 story: how a nation shaped by anticolonial struggle became fertile ground 
 for wealth concentration and enduring inequality. While these contradictio
 ns are not unique to South Africa\, its historical trajectory offers broad
 er insights into persistent disparities across African societies.\n\nI arg
 ue that the rise of privileged minorities—small\, exclusive groups that 
 dominate political and economic life—paralleled the development of succe
 ssful anticolonial movements. These minorities secured exclusive advantage
 s\, defined as benefits and protections that enabled material accumulation
 . Such advantages included land seizures\, racialized labor systems\, acce
 ss to coercive institutions\, favorable regulatory environments\, and targ
 eted state expenditures. Though these mechanisms could foster overall econ
 omic growth\, they disproportionately benefited select oligarchies and mid
 dle classes.\n\nMy work traces how distinct sociocultural groups in South 
 Africa navigated and negotiated these advantages from the Dutch colonial e
 ra through the rise and decline of African National Congress rule. Rather 
 than dismantling minority privilege\, challenges from marginalized groups 
 often served to reshape entrenched advantages by incorporating new actors 
 into existing structures. These dynamics produced composite systems of acc
 umulation that sustained inequality. Through this historical lens\, I offe
 r a new framework for understandin
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54323097577907@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261024T005836Z
DTSTART:20270120T200000Z
DTEND:20270120T210000Z
SUMMARY:[線上] The Spiritual Highway: Religious World Making in Megacity 
 Lagos - Professor Marloes Janson
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-spiritual-highway-religious-world
 -making-in-megacity-lagos-professor-marloes-janson
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54323097577907.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 spiritual-highway-religious-world-making-in-megacity-lagos-professor-marlo
 es-janson\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/H5CFNgAxdF3wekwE6\n\nSession D
 escription:\n\nOpened in 1978 at the height of Nigeria’s oil boom\, the 
 Lagos-Ibadan Expressway was once celebrated as a symbol of progress. Howev
 er\, rising traffic volumes and decades of neglect gradually transformed i
 t into one of the country’s deadliest roads\, earning it the nickname 
 “Highway of Death”. Yet even as it declined as a transport corridor\, 
 it flourished as a stage for public religiosity. Today\, lined with vast p
 rayer camps\, it is widely known as the “Spiritual Highway”. \n\nIn th
 is panel discussion\, photographer Dr Akintunde Akinleye and anthropologis
 t Professor Marloes Janson will explore how religious organizations have e
 merged as influential city-builders in Lagos\, Nigeria’s economic hub. D
 rawing on ethnographic research and photography\, they will show how the L
 agos-Ibadan Expressway has become a key site for understanding the relatio
 nship between religion and urban development. Along this corridor\, religi
 on is not simply a feature of urban life\; it is a constitutive force thro
 ugh which the city itself is made and remade.\n\nSpeaker Biography:\n\nMar
 loes Janson is Professor of West African Anthropology at SOAS University o
 f London. She holds a PhD in Anthropology from Leiden University\, The Net
 herlands. Her publications include Islam\, Youth\, and Modernity in the Ga
 mbia: The Tablighi Jamaʻat (Cambridge University Press\, 2014)\, which ha
 s been awarded the RAI Amaury Talbot Prize for African Anthropology\; Cros
 sing Religious Boundaries: Islam\, Christianity\, and ‘
END:VEVENT
