SUMMARY:[現場] BOOK TALK | The Art of Edmonia Lewis
LOCATION:Roble Gym\, Lounge
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-art-of-edmonia-lewis
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54435335464306.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 art-of-edmonia-lewis\n報名/票券: https://forms.gle/HFYpFXiXH7NtERR18\n
 \nLunch will be served — RSVP Requested.\n\nArt Historian Dr. Kirsten Pa
 i Buick\, author of Child of the Fire—the first major study of the famed
 ​​ Afro-Native neo-classical sculptor Edmonia Lewis (1844-1907)—disc
 usses the artist with TAPS Professor Jennifer DeVere Brody\, whose recent 
 book Moving Stones also centers the work of this remarkable figure whose i
 nternational professional career served as a model.\n\nThis event is spons
 ored by Stanford TAPS\, the Department of African & African American Studi
 es\, the Department of Art and Art History\, and CCSRE's Imagining Justice
 .\n\nABOUT JENNIFER DEVERE BRODY'S BOOK MOVING STONES | Moving Stones expl
 ores the extraordinary life and work of Edmonia Lewis\, the Black and Ojib
 we sculptor who rose to international fame in the nineteenth century. Blen
 ding biography\, history\, and theory\, Jennifer DeVere Brody approaches L
 ewis’s legacy through a Black feminist and queer lens\, illuminating how
  her sculptures and self-fashioning challenged constraints of her time. Li
 ving much of her life in Rome as a free Afro-Native woman\, Lewis used neo
 classical forms to carve out a life in art. Brody considers how Lewis’s 
 works were viewed historically and how they resonate with postmodern artis
 ts\, engaging themes of race\, materiality\, sexuality\, and embodiment. R
 ethinking one of the most important sculptors of her era\, Moving Stones s
 hows how Lewis’s art continues to inspire contemporary artists and schol
 ars today.\n\nABOUT KIRSTEN PAI BUICK | Kirsten Pai Buick is P
END:VEVENT
