SUMMARY:[現場] Poetry on the Porch with Stephanie Horvath and Hieu Minh N
 guyen
LOCATION:Mariposa House\, Front Lawn
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/poetry-on-the-porch-with-stephanie-ho
 rvath-and-hieu-minh-nguyen
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54471654919634.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/poet
 ry-on-the-porch-with-stephanie-horvath-and-hieu-minh-nguyen\n報名/票券
 : https://forms.gle/pTpor2hCFwK8TKn47\n\nJoin us for an afternoon of poetr
 y on the porch\, in celebration of Creative Writing lecturers Stephanie Ho
 rvath and Hieu Minh Nguyen\, as they launch their newest poetry collection
 s Field of Vision and Staying Still.\n\nThis event is open to Stanford aff
 iliates and the general public. Refreshments provided and books available 
 for purchase. Registration is encouraged but not required\; register here.
 \n\n____\n\nIn Horvath's Field of Vision\, we traverse the thresholds of p
 ast and future\, reality and dream\, as we accompany the speaker through d
 eserts\, forests\, rivers\, and coasts. These poems capture\, viscerally a
 nd intimately\, a sense of deep time in the rural landscape—dark\, damp 
 dirt on one’s hands\, a desert over which vultures circle\, the decay of
  wet leaves on a forest floor. A meditation on the horror and resplendence
  of nature\, Field of Vision invokes the ancient history that weaves into 
 each of our individual histories—the earth\, the animals\, the fossils\,
  the primordial echoes that shape us all.\n\nThe highly anticipated follow
 -up to the award-winning poetry collection Not Here\, Nguyen’s Staying S
 till centers on the question of how: How do our anxieties around the idea 
 of belonging estrange us from the very world we seek to belong in? How imp
 ossible does it feel to stay still and face ourselves? From the intimate l
 onging of queer boyhood to the collective expectations imposed upon childr
 en of refugees\, these poems face head-on the rejections\, grief\, and vio
 lence we fear in fracture
END:VEVENT
