SUMMARY:[現場] Overt Listening (2026): A Screening and Conversation with 
 Tiffany Sia
LOCATION:McMurtry Building\, Oshman Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/overt-listening-2026-a-screening-and-
 conversation-with-tiffany-sia
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54481448193530.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/over
 t-listening-2026-a-screening-and-conversation-with-tiffany-sia\n\nStanford
  Cinematheque\, a graduate student-run film collective based in the Depart
 ment of Art & Art History\, presents a screening of Overt Listening\, foll
 owed by a discussion with filmmaker Tiffany Sia.\n\nAbout the Film\nKinmen
  is a cluster of Taiwanese islands several kilometers from the Chinese coa
 stline. It is also a national park. Overt Listening (2026) is Tiffany Sia'
 s debut feature-length film\, an essay film without voiceover that moves t
 hrough Kinmen's pristine shorelines\, roadside bunker attractions\, underg
 round military tunnels\, and traffic circles centering statues of Chiang K
 ai-shek and Sun Yat-sen\, myriad sites preserved by Taiwan's national park
  services that attempt to calcify the territory's Cold War history into sp
 ectacle and memorial. In place of spoken language\, the film works through
  the intertitle—owing to early cinema's grammar of silent film—as an i
 nterstitial voice: at once historical record\, suppressed testimony\, and 
 what the landscape doesn't show. While 1989 through 1991 are commonly rega
 rded as the years global Cold War tensions eased\, Overt Listening works t
 o discern a more difficult-to-read\, ongoing paradigm. The film relies on 
 various aural presences—ranging from silence\, signal\, noise\, and song
 —where military activity is distantly heard but not seen\, and silence i
 s loaded\, itself unpeaceful.\n\nAbout the Filmmaker\nTiffany Sia (b. 1988
 ) is an artist\, filmmaker\, and writer. Sia's films have screened at the 
 New York Film Festival\, Toronto International 
END:VEVENT
