SUMMARY:[混合] CPMH SEMINAR SERIES 'Cutting-edge advances towards persona
 lized mental health'
LOCATION:Lokey Stem Cell Building\, G1161 Large Conference Room
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/cpmh-seminar-series-cutting-edge-adva
 nces-towards-personalized-mental-health
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54508027849181.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/cpmh
 -seminar-series-cutting-edge-advances-towards-personalized-mental-health\n
 線上資訊:\nClick Here to Register Here\n報名/票券: https://docs.go
 ogle.com/forms/d/e/1FAIpQLScxM67Yltrxv2EDImJ6SGaoQqTdSWheWJQjU9SniGQZS6BO1
 Q/viewform\n\nSTANFORD CENTER FOR PRECISION MENTAL HEALTH SEMINAR SERIES  
    \n\nCutting-edge advances towards personalized mental health \n\nFEAUTU
 RED PRESENTATION \n\nTalk Title: Heart-brain interactions driving cardiova
 scular dysfunction \n\nSpeaker: Alexander R. Nectow\, MD\, PhD            
                                     Assistant Professor\, Department of Me
 dicine\, Columbia University \n\nDATE & TIME: Tuesday\, Dec 1\, 2026 | 9:3
 0 - 10:30 AM PST \n\nLOCATION: Lokey Stem Cell Research Building \, G1161 
 Large Conference Room \n\nREGISTER HERE\n\nABOUT THE TALK \n\nStress is a 
 major trigger of arrhythmia\, yet the mechanisms underlying these effects 
 remain poorly defined. Using a genetic model of ventricular tachycardia (V
 T)\, we find that naturalistic emotional stressors rapidly provoke maligna
 nt arrhythmias in vulnerable hearts. By tracing back from sympathetic neur
 ons innervating myocardium\, we identify a multi-synaptic\, brain-to-heart
  circuit originating within the midbrain\, which is required for stress-in
 duced VT. Strikingly\, activation of this pathway induces VT within second
 s\, suggesting that central neuronal control of the heart is an important 
 arrhythmogenic trigger. Together\, this work identifies a brain-to-heart c
 ircuit responsible for triggering stress-induced arrhythmias and points to
 ward circuit-directed therapies for this class of tachyarrhythmias. \n\nAB
 OUT THE SPEAKER \n\nAlexander Nectow\, MD\, PhD\, is an Assistant Professo
 r at Columbia University who has established the dorsal raphe nucleus as a
END:VEVENT
