SUMMARY:[混合] Chinese Railroad Workers\, Leland and Jane Stanford\, and 
 the Founding of the University
LOCATION:CEMEX Auditorium
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/chinese-railroad-workers
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54509116875711.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/chin
 ese-railroad-workers\n報名/票券: https://historicalsociety.stanford.ed
 u/chinese-railroad-workers\n\nThe completion of the first transcontinental
  railroad in 1869 connected the East and West coasts of the United States.
  It was a dramatic engineering\, social\, economic\, and cultural developm
 ent and hailed as a great national accomplishment. Thousands of workers fr
 om China made it possible. Their contribution has been underappreciated an
 d even forgotten. Stanford Professor Gordon Chang will draw on his years o
 f scholarship to recover the story of the Chinese Railroad Workers\, their
  surprising relationship to the Stanfords\, and their important place in t
 he early University.\n\nFree and open to the public. Registration required
 . Free parking at A and C spots after 4 p.m. except after 6 p.m. at the Ov
 al.\n\nRegister to attend in person\nRegister to attend on Zoom\n\n Gordon
  H. Chang is the Olive H. Palmer Professor in Humanities and is Affiliated
  Faculty by courtesy East Asian Languages and Culture. He is interested in
  several different areas of history\, including the historical connections
  between race and ethnicity in America\, on the one hand\, and foreign rel
 ations\, on the other\, and trans-Pacific relations in their diplomatic as
  well as their cultural and social dimensions. He has written and continue
  to publish in the areas of U.S. diplomacy\, America-China relations\, the
  Chinese diaspora\, Asian American history\, and global history. His most 
 recent books have examined the history of Chinese railroad workers in Amer
 ica in the 19th century. His latest book\, War\, Race\, and Culture: Journ
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54511632400099@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261122T081129Z
DTSTART:20270202T200000Z
DTEND:20270202T213000Z
SUMMARY:[線上] The Art of Changing Habits
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-art-of-changing-habits
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54511632400099.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 art-of-changing-habits\n\nHave you ever started a new routine with real de
 termination\, only to slide back into old patterns a few weeks later? Whet
 her you want to change your diet\, limit screen time\, or build a differen
 t daily rhythm\, shifting deeply ingrained habits takes more than willpowe
 r.\n\nJoin us for an interactive online class where you will explore pract
 ical\, compassionate strategies for making change that lasts. You will lea
 rn how to identify your own motivations and the rewards that keep current 
 habits in place\, so you can work with your habits instead of against them
 . You will also practice approaching obstacles with curiosity rather than 
 guilt\, which helps sustain momentum without the discouraging effects of s
 elf-judgment.\n\nTogether\, we will discuss how to link new behaviors to e
 xisting routines and cues in your life\, making them easier to remember an
 d repeat. You will also learn how to break big goals into small\, manageab
 le steps that fit into a busy day.\n\nYou will leave with a set of simple 
 tools to begin shifting the routines that matter most to you and a persona
 l plan to work towards one small change you are ready to make.\n\nThis cla
 ss will not be recorded. \n\nAttendance requirement for incentive points -
  at least 80% of the live session.  \n\nRequest disability accommodations 
 and access info.\n\n\n\nClass details are subject to change.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54511632767757@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261122T081132Z
DTSTART:20270213T000000Z
DTEND:20270213T010000Z
SUMMARY:[線上] The Art and Science of Breathing (February 12 - 26)
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/the-art-and-science-of-breathing-febr
 uary-12-26
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54511632767757.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/the-
 art-and-science-of-breathing-february-12-26\n\nIn this online class\, you 
 will learn breathing techniques to increase energy\, enhance concentration
 \, cultivate compassionate connection to yourself and others\, create rela
 xation\, and deepen sleep. Over three sessions\, we will explore the scien
 ce of breath and the specific benefits of a variety of practices including
  diaphragmatic breathing\, warming versus cooling techniques\, and calming
  and centering skills.\n\nThrough guided experiential practice and informa
 tive multimodal instruction\, you will discover the benefits of a variety 
 of breathing practices and learn how best to incorporate each technique in
 to your daily life. You also will create a personal action plan for enhanc
 ing more effective breathing to enrich your lifelong health and well-being
 .\n\nThis class will not be recorded. \n\nAttendance requirement for incen
 tive points - at least 80% of the live session.  \n\nRequest disability ac
 commodations and access info.\n\nClass details are subject to change.
END:VEVENT
