UID:stanford-54533257378006@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261120T210925Z
DTSTART:20270127T010000Z
DTEND:20270127T023000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Is Journalistic Neutrality Necessary? Dangerous? Impossibl
 e?
LOCATION:Encina Hall\, Bechtel Conference Center
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/is-journalistic-neutrality-necessary-
 dangerous-impossible
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54533257378006.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/is-j
 ournalistic-neutrality-necessary-dangerous-impossible\n報名/票券: http
 s://bit.ly/wesson27\n\nPlease note that this event is in-person only\, and
  RSVPs are requested to attend. Walk-ins are welcome.\n\nRegister now!\n\n
 The 2026-27 Wesson Lecture features Jonah Goldberg and Kelsey Piper discus
 sing the role of journalism in a polarized political landscape.\n\nShould 
 journalists be open about their political views? Should they feel obliged 
 to include facts that conflict with their arguments? How has avowedly poli
 tical journalism changed in the last ten years\, on the right and on the l
 eft? This panel brings together journalists Jonah Goldberg and Kelsey Pipe
 r in conversation with Larissa MacFarquhar (The New Yorker). Goldberg co-f
 ounded The Dispatch as a fact-based media company informed by conservative
  principles\; Piper writes for The Argument\, an outlet dedicated to makin
 g the case for liberalism through in-depth reporting and critique. Togethe
 r\, the panelists will examine how rigorous reporting and normative argume
 nt work together.\n\nSpeakers:\n\n💡 Jonah Goldberg is a senior fellow a
 t AEI\, where he holds the Asness Chair in Applied Liberty. In 2019\, he l
 eft National Review after a 21-year stint to cofound The Dispatch\, where 
 he is editor in chief. He has been a weekly columnist for the Los Angeles 
 Times since 2005 and a nationally syndicated columnist since 2000. He host
 s the popular podcast The Remnant with Jonah Goldberg. Mr. Goldberg is a C
 NN commentator and the author of three New York Times bestsellers. The Atl
 antic has named him one of the top 50 political commentators in 
