 tainable-artificial-intelligence-guided-energy-systems-from-fundamental-sc
 ience
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54550492605028.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/ese-
 seminar-boryann-liaw-building-sustainable-artificial-intelligence-guided-e
 nergy-systems-from-fundamental-science\n\nAbstract\n\nFuture society needs
  a sustainable energy supply to support economic growth\, and artificial i
 ntelligence (AI)-aided\, data-driven approaches are rapidly transforming t
 he energy landscape. Sustaining this momentum requires solutions that are 
 both cost effective and energy efficient. The current approach relies on h
 euristic methods and intensive data acquisition\, but resource constraints
  make rapid deployment difficult. Quantifying returns on investment while 
 accounting for uncertainty and risk adds another major challenge. A trustw
 orthy framework is therefore essential to guide this transformation—but 
 how can such a framework be built?\n\nPhysics-informed framework has been 
 often proposed as a safeguard for the data-driven paradigm. To make this p
 aradigm trustworthy\, however\, its physics-informed foundation must be re
 inforced through fundamental science. This presentation will discuss how e
 mpirical reduction and induction can strengthen that foundation through qu
 antification and verification. The goal is to ensure that regression-model
  predictions not only obey fundamental principles but are also quantitativ
 ely interpretable. This would connect controlled laboratory test data with
  real-world\, live data streams for practical applications. Because limite
 d data transferability has become a major bottleneck\, a quantitative and 
 verifiable physics-informed framework could help overcome it and accelerat
 e a sustainable transformation that is both cost-effective and energy-effi
 c
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54551038616750@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261118T192438Z
DTSTART:20261208T230000Z
DTEND:20261209T000000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] eWEAR Seminar: AI-XR Co-Scientist
LOCATION:Spilker\, 232
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/ewear-seminar-ai-xr-co-scientist
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54551038616750.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/ewea
 r-seminar-ai-xr-co-scientist\n線上連結: https://stanford.zoom.us/webin
 ar/register/2717950289779/WN_57V-PytjQa6j6iANRmmTUA\n報名/票券: https:
 //stanford.zoom.us/webinar/register/2717950289779/WN_57V-PytjQa6j6iANRmmTU
 A\n\n“LabOS: The AI-XR Co-Scientist that Sees and Works with Humans”\n
 \nSpeaker: Mengdi Wang\, Professor of Electrical\, Computer Engineering\, 
 the Center for Statistics and Machine Learning\; and Founding Co-director 
 of Princeton AI for Accelerated Invention\, Princeton University\n\nAbstra
 ct: Modern science advances fastest when thought meets action. LabOS repre
 sents the first AI co-scientist that unites computational reasoning with p
 hysical experimentation through multimodal perception\, self-evolving agen
 ts\, and Extended-Reality(XR)-enabled human-AI collaboration. By connectin
 g multi-model AI agents\, smart glasses\, and robots\, LabOS allows AI to 
 see what scientists see\, understand experimental context\, and assist in 
 real-time execution. Across applications — from cancer immunotherapy tar
 get discovery to stem-cell engineering and material science — LabOS show
 s that AI can move beyond computational design to participation\, turning 
 the laboratory into an intelligent\, collaborative environment where human
  and machine discovery evolve together.\n\nBio: Mengdi Wang is Co-Director
  of Princeton AI for Accelerated Invention\, and Professor of the Departme
 nt of Electrical and Computer Engineering and the Center for Statistics an
 d Machine Learning at Princeton University. She is also affiliated with th
 e Department of Computer Science\, Omenn-Darling Bioengineering Institute\
 , and Princeton Language+Intelligence. She was a visiting research scienti
 st at Google DeepMind\, IAS and Simons Institute on Theor
END:VEVENT
