DTEND:20270226T200000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Book Talk: Your Presence Is Mandatory with Sasha Vasilyuk
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/book-talk-your-presence-is-mandatory-
 with-sasha-vasilyuk
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54551728906362.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/book
 -talk-your-presence-is-mandatory-with-sasha-vasilyuk\n\nTalk Details:\nSpa
 nning 70 years between World War II and Russia’s war in Ukraine\, Your P
 resence Is Mandatory tells the story of a Ukrainian Jewish war veteran wit
 h a dangerous lifelong secret\, the repercussions for his family\, and the
  grace they find in the course of their survival.\n\nRSVP here.\n\nAbout t
 he Speaker:\nSasha Vasilyuk is a journalist and author of a debut novel\, 
 Your Presence Is Mandatory (Bloomsbury\, 2024)\, winner of the California 
 Book Award and the Sami Rohr Prize for Jewish Literature. The novel was al
 so longlisted for the Center for Fiction First Novel Prize and translated 
 into seven languages. Her nonfiction has been published in the New York Ti
 mes\, CNN\, Harper’s Bazaar\, Time\, USA Today\, Telegraph\, and elsewhe
 re. Sasha grew up between Ukraine and Russia before immigrating to the U.S
 . at the age of 13. She earned a BA in Comparative Literature from UC Berk
 eley and MA in Journalism from New York University.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54560548123527@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261119T212435Z
DTSTART:20261201T013000Z
DTEND:20261201T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Faculty Candidate Talk: “The Acoustics of Accented Labor
 : Genevieve Nnaji and Industrial Authorship in LIONHEART (2018)” | Rejoi
 ce Abutsa\, Doctoral Candidate\, Cornell University
LOCATION:McMurtry Building\, Oshman Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/faculty-candidate-talk-the-acoustics-
 of-accented-labor-genevieve-nnaji-and-industrial-authorship-in-lionheart-2
 018-rejoice-abutsa-doctoral-candidate-cornell-university
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54560548123527.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/facu
 lty-candidate-talk-the-acoustics-of-accented-labor-genevieve-nnaji-and-ind
 ustrial-authorship-in-lionheart-2018-rejoice-abutsa-doctoral-candidate-cor
 nell-university\n\nBlack women’s presence\, participation\, and labor ha
 ve been central to Nollywood’s formation and global circulation\, even a
 s their contributions remain marginalized in critical scholarship. On-scre
 en and behind the scenes\, women have shaped Nollywood’s emergence as th
 e world’s second-largest film industry by annual production volume. Situ
 ated at the intersection of African cultural production\, women’s cinema
 \, and global Black media\, this talk examines women’s paradoxical posit
 ioning within Nigerian cinema through one of Africa’s most prominent fil
 m stars\, Genevieve Nnaji. Her directorial debut\, Lionheart (2018)\, rais
 es critical questions about women’s creative labor\, authorship\, and in
 dustrial politics\, particularly how women filmmakers navigate\, challenge
 \, and transform the institutional structures that determine African cinem
 a’s visibility and legitimacy on the global stage. Combining textual and
  industrial analysis\, the talk examines how Lionheart deploys a multiling
 ual acoustic landscape to explore the politics of language\, listening\, a
 nd belonging. In doing so\, I demonstrate how Nnaji’s aesthetic and indu
 strial interventions illuminate the complex relationship between women’s
  creative authority\, Nigerian cultural identity\, and the global circulat
 ion of African cinema.\n\nRejoice Abutsa is a filmmaker\, programmer\, and
  doctoral candidate in the Department of Performing and Media Arts at Corn
 ell University. Working at the intersection of global Black media studies\
 , women’s cin
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54560574222899@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261119T212435Z
DTSTART:20270122T200000Z
DTEND:20270122T200000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Praying for Victory: Loyalty\, Dissent\, and Repression in
 side the Russian Orthodox Church Today
LOCATION:Encina Commons\, Room 123
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/praying-for-victory-loyalty-dissent-a
 nd-repression-inside-the-russian-orthodox-church-today
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54560574222899.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/pray
 ing-for-victory-loyalty-dissent-and-repression-inside-the-russian-orthodox
 -church-today\n\nTalk Details:\nSince 2022 the Moscow Patriarchate has dem
 anded more of its clergy than silence. Every liturgy now includes a prayer
  for “Holy Rus’” that asks God to grant victory\, and priests who re
 placed “victory” with “peace” have been suspended or defrocked. Be
 lievers who speak against the war face church courts as well as prosecutio
 n for “discrediting” the army\, sometimes after their own parishioners
  have reported them.\n\nNone of this began in 2022. Over three post-Soviet
  decades the church assembled a language of loyalty piece by piece: the my
 ths of “Holy Rus’” and the “Russian world\,” a memory of the Sec
 ond World War recast in sacred terms\, St. Seraphim of Sarov adopted as pa
 tron of the nuclear weapons complex. Alongside the myths came an imagery t
 hat borrows the authority of the icon while emptying it of meaning. In 202
 0 the Main Cathedral of the Russian Armed Forces gathered all of it into o
 ne building. Drawing on cases documented between 2022 and 2026 and on fift
 een years of work inside the Patriarchate\, Sergei Chapnin traces how this
  apparatus of images and myths was built\, how it now produces and polices
  loyalty\, and what dissent looks like when open protest is impossible. Wh
 at does this crisis reveal about the church’s bond with state power\, an
 d what does it leave to Orthodox Christians in Russia and abroad?\n\nRSVP 
 here\n\nAbout the Speaker:\nSergei Chapnin is a Russian Orthodox journalis
 t\, scholar\, and editor who has written on the Russian Orthodox Church\, 
 church–state
END:VEVENT
