SUMMARY:[現場] German Studies Lecture Series: Hannes Bajohr - Worlds in A
 I and the Novel
LOCATION:Building 260\, Pigott Hall\, Rm 216
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/german-studies-lecture-series-hannes-
 bajohr-worlds-in-ai-and-the-novel
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54561022405627.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/germ
 an-studies-lecture-series-hannes-bajohr-worlds-in-ai-and-the-novel\n\nPlea
 se join German Studies Lecture Series for Worlds in AI and the Novel\, a c
 onversation with Hannes Bajohr (UC Berkeley).\n\n“A world—nothing less
 —is the theme and postulate of the novel\,” Hans Blumenberg wrote in 1
 963\, as AI research was beginning to develop its own “world models.” 
 Today\, these literary and computational senses of world converge in LLMs\
 , whose latent spaces can generate narratives with striking coherence but 
 often weak causality. Drawing on literary traditions from realism to moder
 nism and on his own experiments with a fine-tuned German-language model\, 
 Professor Bajohr asks how novels and LLMs construct worlds through network
 s of relations\, inferences\, and expectations. The talk uses this compari
 son to situate AI-generated fiction between claims of machine understandin
 g and dismissals of mere mimicry.\n\nRSVP for the Hannes Bajohr event here
 .
END:VEVENT
