UID:stanford-54561153360474@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261119T230235Z
DTSTART:20261203T013000Z
DTEND:20261203T030000Z
SUMMARY:[現場] Faculty Candidate Talk: “Sentinel and Storyteller: Black
  Women’s Cinematic Memory Work” | Yasmina Price\, Presidential Postdoc
 toral Research Fellow\, Princeton University
LOCATION:McMurtry Building\, Oshman Hall
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/faculty-candidate-talk-sentinel-and-s
 toryteller-black-womens-cinematic-memory-work-yasmina-price-presidential-p
 ostdoctoral-research-fellow-princeton-university
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54561153360474.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/facu
 lty-candidate-talk-sentinel-and-storyteller-black-womens-cinematic-memory-
 work-yasmina-price-presidential-postdoctoral-research-fellow-princeton-uni
 versity\n\nAt the interstices of transnational Black feminism and Third Ci
 nema\, filmmakers Sarah Maldoror and Sara Gómez authored an alternative s
 tory of liberation. Both contributors to the cultural front of anticolonia
 l militancy and class struggle in the 1960s-70s\, they recast prevailing n
 ationalist and masculinist norms. Their cinematic memory work of witnessin
 g and retelling illuminates a meaningful Black feminist genealogy within A
 frican and Afro-diasporic cinemas. Through hybrid and polyvocal short docu
 mentaries\, Maldoror and Gómez produced a kinetic archive\, which demands
  a critical\, pluralistic recovery through the interdependence of scholars
 hip\, public programming and material restoration. Revisiting Maldoror and
  Gómez situates Black women’s aesthetic\, political and theoretical eng
 agements with history and visuality carried forward by contemporary filmma
 kers and visual artists.\n\nYasmina Price is a writer\, film programmer an
 d scholar of African & Afro-diasporic visual culture. Her work pays partic
 ular attention to the materiality of image-making\, aesthetic historiograp
 hy and the experimental practices of women filmmakers and artists. Her wri
 ting has appeared in Film Quarterly\, World Records Journal\, The Nation\,
  frieze\, Criterion’s Current and elsewhere. Her programming and curator
 ial work includes Anthology Film Archives\, the Brooklyn Academy of Music\
 , the Schomburg Center for Research in Black Culture\, the Los Angeles Cou
 nty Museum of Art and the Pan African Film & Arts Festiv
END:VEVENT
