SUMMARY:[現場] Three L.A.S.E.R. talks: Astrophysics\, Biology\, AI
LOCATION:Li Ka Shing Center\, LK 120
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/three-laser-talks-astrophysics-biolog
 y-ai
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54561477025888.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/thre
 e-laser-talks-astrophysics-biology-ai\n\nThe LASERs (Leonardo Art Science 
 Evening Rendezvous) are an international program of evening gatherings tha
 t bring artists and scientists together for informal presentations and con
 versation with an audience. This evening\, chaired by cultural historian P
 iero Scaruffi\, will feature these talks:\n\n- Roger Blandford (Stanford/P
 hysics) on "Extreme Physics from Black Holes and Neutron Stars”\n\n- Ste
 phen Quake (Stanford) on "How AI is Changing Our Understanding of Cell Bio
 logy"\n\n- Hermann Nyuykonge Kumbong (Stanford) on “Beyond Autoregressio
 n: Next-Concept Modeling”\n\nThe art component will consist of a lunch-t
 ime Zoom conversation about the poet Fernando Pessoa with Portugal-based s
 cholar Richard Zenith for which you can register at: L.A.S.E.R.: A convers
 ation about poet Fernando Pessoa - Stanford University\n\nRoger Blandford 
 is the Director of the Simons Collaboration on Extreme Electrodynamics of 
 Compact Sources at Stanford. His research interests include compact source
 s\, cosmic rays\, cosmology\, and astrobiology. He is a Fellow of the Roya
 l Society\, the American Academy of Arts and Sciences\, the American Physi
 cal Society and a Member of the National Academy of Sciences. He was award
 ed the 1998 AAS Heineman Prize \, the 2013 RAS Gold Medal\, the 2016 Crafo
 ord Prize for Astronomy and the 2020 Shaw Prize for Astronomy.\n\nHermann 
 Nyuykonge Kumbong\, originally from Cameroon\, is pursuing a PhD in comput
 er science at Stanford School of Engineering. His research has been publis
 hed at top AI co
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54561756343146@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261121T113431Z
DTSTART:20261130T170000Z
DTEND:20261130T180000Z
SUMMARY:[混合] Geophysics Master's Presentation\, Hamad Alswaidan: "Machi
 ne Learning-Assisted Full Waveform Inversion: Low-Frequency Extrapolation 
 and Cycle-Skipping Mitigation"
LOCATION:Mitchell Earth Sciences\, 350/372
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/geophysics-masters-presentation-hamad
 -alswaidan-machine-learning-assisted-full-waveform-inversion-low-frequency
 -extrapolation-and-cycle-skipping-mitigation
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54561756343146.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/geop
 hysics-masters-presentation-hamad-alswaidan-machine-learning-assisted-full
 -waveform-inversion-low-frequency-extrapolation-and-cycle-skipping-mitigat
 ion\n線上資訊:\nhttps://stanford.zoom.us/j/93984894466?pwd=2VqzQGageoy
 JcqQOp7vzVWzXr7DojF.1\n\nFull waveform inversion (FWI) is a powerful techn
 ique for estimating subsurface physical properties from seismic data. Howe
 ver\, its nonlinear nature and the limited availability of low-frequency s
 eismic data make it susceptible to cycle skipping\, often preventing conve
 rgence toward an accurate subsurface model.\n\nIn this work\, Hamad explor
 es two complementary machine learning approaches to address these challeng
 es. The first investigates the use of generative diffusion models to extra
 polate missing low-frequency content in seismic data. The second introduce
 s a neural network-based approach to mitigate cycle skipping through the a
 lignment of observed and modeled seismic waveforms during inversion.\n\nTo
 gether\, these approaches explore how machine learning can complement phys
 ics-based inversion and improve the robustness of FWI.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:stanford-54561882485995@didiowen
DTSTAMP:20261120T010325Z
DTSTART:20261202T180000Z
DTEND:20261202T193000Z
SUMMARY:[線上] Digital Humanities in Slavic Studies - Nataliia Laba: Pol
 itical Economy of Grief: AI-generated Media and Commercialization of War
LOCATION:
URL:https://events.stanford.edu/event/digital-humanities-in-slavic-studies-
 nataliialaba-political-economy-of-grief
DESCRIPTION:➕ 加到我的行事曆: https://didiowen.github.io/stanford-
 public-events/e/54561882485995.ics\nhttps://events.stanford.edu/event/digi
 tal-humanities-in-slavic-studies-nataliialaba-political-economy-of-grief\n
 \nPolitical economy of grief: AI-generated media and commercialization of 
 war\nIf representation is an act of conferring visibility\, this power is 
 now challenged by visual generative AI and highly realistic synthetic imag
 es of war and conflict. So far\, research on visual generative AI in the c
 ontext of war and conflict representation has mostly focused on the repres
 entational implications of AI-generated images and their role in adding fu
 el to information disorder. In this talk\, I shift attention to the techno
 logical infrastructures of image generators and stock image sites that inc
 entivize and at times blatantly encourage curation of such images. By unpa
 cking the relationship between the production\, representation and circula
 tion of AI-generated images of the Russia-Ukraine war\, I discuss methodol
 ogical challenges and potential solutions to studying AI-mediated commerci
 alization and visual construction of war. \n\nBio: Nataliia Laba is an Ass
 istant Professor in digital and multimodal communication / humane AI at th
 e University of Groningen in the Netherlands. She has written on AI-genera
 ted images and war representation\, sociotechnical imaginaries of visual g
 enerative AI and AGI\, and visual AI style\, from critical data studies an
 d communication perspectives. She is a co-editor of Six Critical Lenses on
  AI-Generated Images (CRC Press\, 2026). https://nataliialaba.com/ \n\nReg
 ister for the talk over Zoom with Nataliia Laba
END:VEVENT
